WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.080
If you happened to stumble upon any&nbsp;
of the numerous Predator films without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:08.240
seeing all the ones that came before,&nbsp;
you might come away confused. Here's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.320
what each entry in the "Predator" series&nbsp;
means in the context of the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.960
including what it adds to&nbsp;
the bigger-picture mythos.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.320
"Predator" follows an elite team of mercenaries&nbsp;
on a rescue mission deep in the heart of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.440
guerilla-controlled area in Central America.&nbsp;
The team, led by ex-special forces Major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.960
Alan "Dutch" Schaefer, quickly finds unexpected&nbsp;
helicopter wreckage, a slew of skinned corpses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.840
and evidence of a killer that leaves no tracks.&nbsp;
They make quick work of a group of insurgents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.040
taking one unarmed female&nbsp;
insurgent, Anna, captive.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.560
As the mercenary team moves forward, the unseen&nbsp;
foe starts to pick off members one by one.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:45.920
"You ain't afraid of no man!"

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:52.320
"There's something out there waiting&nbsp;
for us. And it ain't no man."

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.120
Anna tells Dutch of a local legend: a&nbsp;
creature that hunts humans for sport.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:03.120
Eventually only Dutch and Anna remain, and Dutch&nbsp;
realizes the creature only targets armed prey:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.880
he sends Anna off alone so&nbsp;
she'll be safe from its attacks.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.720
Dutch becomes covered in mud, discovering&nbsp;
that the mud allows him to hide from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.720
the entity as it moves on. Dutch is&nbsp;
able to damage its cloaking device,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.920
but he is still overcome by the creature, AKA&nbsp;
the Predator, more accurately known as a Yautja.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.720
It drops its armor and weapons for a&nbsp;
fair fight, and Dutch nearly loses before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.680
catching the Predator in a trap. The fallen&nbsp;
Yautja activates a self-destruct device.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.520
Dutch narrowly escapes, fleeing just&nbsp;
before the creature's self-destruction.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.200
As our introduction to the species, "Predator"&nbsp;
demonstrates a number of characteristics of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.440
the creature. Visually, they're adorned&nbsp;
in seemingly light but powerful armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.800
and have menacingly tough alien bodies.They&nbsp;
have a high-technology spacefaring civilization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.200
with an advanced and distinct language,&nbsp;
deadly weapons, cloaking technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.480
and they're smart, strong, adaptive&nbsp;
warriors who can spot and create traps.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:02.560
The Yaujta are here killing armed&nbsp;
prey for sport, often hunt alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.200
skin their quarry, and take trophies. If&nbsp;
their hunt fails and they're facing death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.720
they'd rather kill themselves than&nbsp;
lose. At this point in the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:15.600
we can only infer that such a hunt was the&nbsp;
Predator's sole motivation for traveling to Earth.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:22.000
From the film we learn that the Yautja actually&nbsp;
enjoy the challenge, and also exhibit a menacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.400
joy in getting that last one-up kill on&nbsp;
their prey. The film effectively set the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.040
tone for every subsequent portrayal of the&nbsp;
entity, and its legacy affects the series.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.000
The film received mixed critical reviews in its&nbsp;
day but its box office success was undeniable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.160
netting $98 million on a&nbsp;
production budget of $18 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:44.320
The film cemented the Predators as an&nbsp;
iconic movie monster species, fueling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:49.840
20th Century Fox to greenlight numerous direct&nbsp;
and spin-off sequels over subsequent decades.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.520
The second film shifts the action into urban&nbsp;
territory. A gang war brings the involvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:01.360
of LAPD Lieutenant Mike Harrigan and his team.&nbsp;
The cops barge into a hideout to find a Colombian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.080
gang slaughtered, and Harrigan follows the&nbsp;
panicked survivor to the roof to see the vague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.800
transparent silhouette of a large,&nbsp;
semi-invisible man. At his precinct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:16.640
Harrigan is introduced to Special Agent Keyes, the&nbsp;
leader of a task force investigating the cartels.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:21.280
That night, another gangland killing is&nbsp;
interrupted by the Predator. Harrigan and his team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.640
investigate the scene of the crime and find an&nbsp;
alien spear. Keyes enters and threatens Harrigan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.920
to stay away, but Harrigan returns and pursues&nbsp;
the Predator. He's captured by Keyes' team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.920
who explains that the Predator is a camouflaged&nbsp;
alien hunter who prowls areas with armed conflict.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:45.840
"Drawn by heat and conflict. He's on safari.&nbsp;
Lions, the tigers, the bears. Oh my."

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:47.760
"Trophies."

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:54.000
Keyes' team sets a trap, but they're easily&nbsp;
overcome by the Yautja, and Harrigan pursues the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.800
alien himself. The fight between the Predator&nbsp;
and Harrigan continues downward into tunnels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.360
beneath the city, where Harrigan discovers&nbsp;
the Yautja spacecraft and its skull-adorned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.320
trophy room — which happens to include a skull&nbsp;
that looks like it belongs to a Xenomorph from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.200
the "Alien" franchise. He manages to kill the&nbsp;
Yautja with its own weapon, causing a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.600
of Predators to appear, collect the fallen&nbsp;
alien, and present Harrigan with a trophy.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:23.920
"Predator 2" made a number of important leaps for&nbsp;
our understanding of the mythos of the Predators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.880
First, it widened the scope of the types of&nbsp;
warrior-combatant prey we know Predators hunt, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.920
showed that they're as willing to hunt in crowded&nbsp;
city centers as they are in remote combat zones.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:39.440
While the film added new weapons to their arsenal,&nbsp;
it also shows that lone-hunter Yautja don't travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.160
to a planet unobserved. Other hunters will&nbsp;
take the body of a fallen comrade 'home'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.000
upon its failure to self-destruct, a&nbsp;
unique evolution of their culture that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:53.120
adds significant ritual to their hunts. The&nbsp;
film also explains the species' propensity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:58.000
to travel to this planet to hunt humans&nbsp;
for sport, particularly in combat zones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.520
and that at least some groups in the&nbsp;
government are aware of the predation.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.360
Finally, in showing the ship's trophy&nbsp;
room and the Xenomorph skull within it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.280
it suggests that the "Alien" and "Predator"&nbsp;
worlds take place in the same universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:14.080
and that the Yautja hunt Xenomorphs too, the&nbsp;
first cinematic introduction of that concept.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:20.160
"Predator 2" added to the Yautja's weaponry&nbsp;
and tactical prowess, implicitly provided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:25.920
the intellectual underpinning for the "Alien vs.&nbsp;
Predator" spin-off films and evolved our Yautja&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.720
cultural understanding: showing their entire&nbsp;
alien civilization is built around the hunt.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.200
Despite the film's current cult classic status,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:38.080
"Predator 2" suffered mostly negative reviews&nbsp;
in its day and fared much more poorly at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:43.040
box office than its predecessor. The film's&nbsp;
relatively lackluster performance at the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:49.040
effectively paused the franchise until the "Alien&nbsp;
vs. Predator" spin off franchise hit in 2004.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.400
In short, "Predator 2" proved mighty influential&nbsp;
in its cementing many aspects of Predator lore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.720
but its relative lack of success paused&nbsp;
the individual franchise for some time.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.880
In 2004's "Alien vs. Predator," wealthy&nbsp;
industrialist Charles Bishop Weyland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:10.400
assembles a team to investigate a mysterious heat&nbsp;
signature beneath an island north of Antarctica.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:15.280
Meanwhile, a Predator ship reaches Earth's orbit&nbsp;
and fires an energy beam straight at the heat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:20.160
signature's location, creating a 'tunnel'&nbsp;
to its source: an ancient pyramid. The team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.520
finds the tunnel as well as evidence of ancient&nbsp;
humans in what seem to be sacrificial chambers.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.040
The team unintentionally activates&nbsp;
devices within the pyramid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.080
reanimating a Xenomorph Queen from stasis,&nbsp;
who produces a host of Xenomorph eggs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.640
Facehuggers attach to humans trapped in the&nbsp;
sacrificial chamber, quickly maturing into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:44.400
adult Xenomorphs. Conflict erupts and escalates&nbsp;
between the Xenomorphs, humans, and the Yautja,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:48.960
and one of the Yautja — known as "Scar"&nbsp;
— finds himself attacked by a facehugger.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.240
Hieroglyph translations reveal that Predators have&nbsp;
been visiting Earth for thousands of years and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.440
were once worshipped as gods. They periodically&nbsp;
return as part of a hunting ritual where humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:05.200
are sacrificed to produce Xenomorphs as prey.&nbsp;
Weyland team guide Lex and Scar form an alliance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.280
planting Scar's self-destruct device&nbsp;
in the Xenomorph hive to destroy the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:14.320
remaining Xenomorphs and eggs. The Queen&nbsp;
survives, but the pair ultimately defeat her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:18.960
although Scar dies from wounds received&nbsp;
in battle. A Predator ship uncloaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:24.320
and the Yautja present Lex with a spear, then&nbsp;
take Scar's body before ascending. The film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:29.840
ends as we watch a Predalien, a combination&nbsp;
of Predators and Aliens, burst from his chest.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:34.320
"Alien vs. Predator" is notably the&nbsp;
first film to establish that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.480
Predators have been hunting on Earth for&nbsp;
thousands of years. And more than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:41.440
they were instrumental in the&nbsp;
earliest human civilizations'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.240
and were a major factor in the development of&nbsp;
early human culture and, possibly, religion.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.640
We also find out Earth is at least&nbsp;
one special location where Yautja&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.080
rites of passage were conducted&nbsp;
to facilitate the Xenomorph hunt.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:00.960
Long treated as non-canon, the two-film "Alien&nbsp;
vs. Predator" series introduces a variety of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:06.160
aspects to both "Predator" and "Alien" mythos.&nbsp;
While "Predator 2" suggested the two species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:11.760
share the same universe, it took a detail in&nbsp;
2018's "The Predator" to subtly reinforce it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:16.960
In that movie, Casey Bracket is shown the&nbsp;
Alien tail Combi-spear crafted for Lex in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:22.560
"AVP," implicitly confirming their canonicity&nbsp;
in the official "Predator" universe. Though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:27.200
given Ridley Scott's antipathy towards the&nbsp;
crossover and the "Prometheus" and "Alien:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:32.240
Covenant" detail differences, it's safe to say&nbsp;
they're not canon in the "Alien" franchise.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.840
Additionally, despite its critical&nbsp;
detractors, the film performed quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:41.840
well at the box office with $172 million&nbsp;
worldwide on a $70 million budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:45.840
breathing new life into the "Predator"&nbsp;
and "Alien" franchises. That said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:51.040
its influence is nonetheless still controversial&nbsp;
— for "Predator" purposes we can safely say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:54.960
that the current state of the franchise&nbsp;
validates its additions to Predator lore.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:01.760
"AVP: Requiem" begins exactly where "AVP"&nbsp;
left off, with a hatched Predalien emerging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:07.120
from Scar's fallen body, rampaging through the&nbsp;
craft and crashing it outside a Colorado town.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:11.200
The ship's distress signal is received&nbsp;
by the elite Predator Wolf, who travels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:16.320
to Earth to eradicate the Predalien and any&nbsp;
facehuggers that escaped. On Earth, a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:21.600
of facehuggers find hosts, while Wolf lands and&nbsp;
starts to clean up evidence of the alien menace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:27.280
Wolf tracks a number of Xenomorphs into the town&nbsp;
sewers, and several escape into the town above.

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:31.440
Meanwhile, several humans regroup at&nbsp;
a local sporting goods store. Wolf and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:35.680
Xenomorphs enter and battle inside the&nbsp;
store as the surviving humans escape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:40.480
The survivors contact the military and are&nbsp;
directed to the center of town for evacuation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:45.840
The human survivors split off, with some moving to&nbsp;
use the hospital's helicopter to escape the area.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:50.480
The hospital splinter group finds the building&nbsp;
overrun by Xenomorphs and the Predalien,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:55.920
and Wolf arrives to kill a number of the Xenos,&nbsp;
losing a blaster in the process. The humans grab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:01.280
the blaster, and conflict rages between them, the&nbsp;
Xenomorphs, and Wolf. The human survivors fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:06.560
their way to the roof, board the chopper, and take&nbsp;
off — all while Wolf and the Predalien engage in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:11.920
final combat on the roof, mortally wounding each&nbsp;
other. Meanwhile, the military has lied about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:17.440
evacuation and instead bombs the city to eliminate&nbsp;
the alien threat. The shockwave crashes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:23.120
escaping hospital helicopter, where the remaining&nbsp;
survivors are rescued by awaiting military forces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:27.920
Soon, Wolf's recovered plasma blaster is&nbsp;
presented to a woman named Ms. Yutani.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:32.160
Building directly off its&nbsp;
predecessor, "Alien vs. Predator:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.880
Requiem" shows that Predators will call on&nbsp;
the assistance of others in case of emergency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:41.120
and that the species will sometimes attempt&nbsp;
to clean up evidence of their activities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:46.000
Additionally, they seem intent on preventing&nbsp;
Xenomorphs from escaping to dominate a planet. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:51.920
also see Wolf use weapons that we have not seen&nbsp;
in use by other Yautja, including the Yautja Whip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:56.400
laser mines, and the Power Punch Glove that&nbsp;
amplifies the Predator's punching power.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.800
Finally, we see an extraterrestrial&nbsp;
plasma pistol given to Ms. Yutani&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:04.480
of the Yutani Corporation. The&nbsp;
company's 2099 merger with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:09.120
Weyland Corp produced the Weyland-Yutani&nbsp;
Corporation we see in the "Alien" films.

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:13.360
Like "AVP," whether or not&nbsp;
'Requiem's' contributions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:17.360
are canon hinges entirely on the&nbsp;
overall status of the spinoffs.

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:21.200
The film was overall received more&nbsp;
poorly than any other "Predator" film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:25.600
yet it earned a higher profit margin&nbsp;
than the preceding spinoff at a $128&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:30.880
million take on a $40 million budget. The&nbsp;
near-universally poor critical reception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:35.440
put the directors' plans for a third&nbsp;
spinoff on indefinite hold. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.920
its contributions to Yautja weaponry and&nbsp;
culture have stayed with the franchise.

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:47.120
"Predators" begins with mercenary Royce waking&nbsp;
up while parachuting into an unfamiliar jungle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:51.920
alongside several other humans. As it turns&nbsp;
out, they're all armed and lethal killers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:57.760
except for one, a doctor named Edwin. When night&nbsp;
comes, they see an alien sky, for the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:01.840
realizing they're no longer on Earth. The&nbsp;
makeshift teammates soon find themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:06.080
attacked by four-legged extraterrestrial&nbsp;
beasts before a whistle calls them off.

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:11.120
The team discovers a captive Predator, but are&nbsp;
soon attacked by three massive "Super Predators,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:16.640
known as the Tracker, Berserker, and Falconer.&nbsp;
The human team flees, and eventually encounter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:21.840
Ronald Noland, a lone U.S. soldier who survived&nbsp;
on the alien planet by looting and hiding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:26.720
Noland reveals the planet is a Predator game&nbsp;
reserve where Yautja train against deadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:32.480
warriors and creatures. There, the Predators hunt&nbsp;
in threes, and the smaller and larger Yautja feud.

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:36.000
"The larger ones hunt the&nbsp;
smaller ones it's some kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:39.920
blood feud I guess, been&nbsp;
going on for a long time."

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:45.680
Conflict between the Predators and the humans&nbsp;
eventually kills Noland, several team members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:51.280
and the Tracker and Falconer Yautja. Edwin becomes&nbsp;
injured and Isabelle refuses to leave him, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:56.480
Royce moves on to free the captured Yautja in&nbsp;
hope of escape. The smaller Yautja activates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:02.000
a ship and programs its return to Earth. The&nbsp;
Berserker arrives, kills the smaller Yautja,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:06.880
and blows up the ship. Royce returns for the&nbsp;
others and discovers Edwin has revealed himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:11.920
to have been a serial killer back on Earth.&nbsp;
Knowing Edwin's far more than an innocent doctor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:16.720
Royce uses him as grenade-trapped bait. The&nbsp;
Berserker triggers the trap, and Royce and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:21.760
Isabelle kill it. The film ends with the pair&nbsp;
looking at the alien sky as more parachutes fall.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:27.360
"Predators" adds a large array of elements&nbsp;
to the mythology of the Predator universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:32.000
First, and perhaps most notably, is that&nbsp;
the Predator species has two distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:37.680
subspecies with a long history of antagonism and a&nbsp;
considerable size difference. The smaller group is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:43.280
often referred to as the Jungle Hunter Clan, while&nbsp;
the Super Predators are larger and more muscular.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:45.920
We also learn that while Jungle Hunter Yautja&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:50.640
frequently travel across the galaxy to&nbsp;
hunt various prey, Super Predator Yautja&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:55.680
have a planet of unknown location where they&nbsp;
regularly deposit and hunt dangerous species.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:59.920
This is also the first instance in the film&nbsp;
series where Yautja are seen hunting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:05.200
quadrupedal animals, a facet of their hunting&nbsp;
culture that hasn't yet been shown on screen.

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:10.320
Finally, there's the case of Edwin — the Yautja&nbsp;
have long been known to hunt armed combatants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:15.440
in combat zones, situations where predation&nbsp;
is easily visible for a Predator to track,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:20.640
find, and hunt armed prey. With Edwin, we discover&nbsp;
that the Predators' methods of reconnaissance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:24.720
are much more sophisticated, as they were&nbsp;
able to discover that he was a killer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:29.840
despite his methods being less visible than the&nbsp;
armed combatants that Predators typically hunt.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:33.600
With "Predators," Fox reinvigorated the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:39.120
reorienting it away from the "AVP" spinoffs&nbsp;
and towards new individual "Predator" outings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:44.720
The film added a lot to the mythos: an entirely&nbsp;
new race of Yautja, a game reserve planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:49.760
a new level of detail in the Yautja's search&nbsp;
for deadly combatants, and hunting animals.

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:54.880
The film grossed $127 million on a $40&nbsp;
million budget and received the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:59.600
acclaim in the film franchise since the&nbsp;
1987 original. Super Predators have not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:03.600
appeared in a subsequent film, but the&nbsp;
film's cinematic sequel "The Predator"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:08.160
does contain a massive Predator with&nbsp;
genetically modified DNA. It's unclear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:12.543
however, if there is a connection between the&nbsp;
Super Predators and that film's massive Yautja.

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:18.880
"The Predator" opens with one Yautja ship&nbsp;
fleeing another before crash landing on Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:23.920
interrupting Army Ranger Quinn McKenna's&nbsp;
mission. The alien attacks his team before he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:26.560
incapacitates it with its weaponry and flees with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:31.200
Yautja armor. McKenna manages to send&nbsp;
the technology home. When he returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:36.560
he's interrogated by agents from Project&nbsp;
Stargazer and put on a military prison transport.

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:40.080
Biologist Casey Bracket&nbsp;
arrives at a Stargazer research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:45.920
lab to investigate the captured Predator body and&nbsp;
the inexplicably spliced DNA its body contains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:50.240
The Predator wakes up, rampages, and&nbsp;
flees. McKenna and other soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:54.800
hijack their prison transport, meet up with&nbsp;
Bracket, and proceed to McKenna's house.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:16:00.160
They discover that McKenna's autistic son Rory&nbsp;
had found the Yautja armor and left the house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:04.800
actually wearing it. McKenna and the soldiers&nbsp;
eventually find the boy before the Predator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:08.320
and the three attempt to give the&nbsp;
Predator its armor back before a larger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:12.960
more powerful Predator emerges and kills&nbsp;
the first. The humans flee, and Bracket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:17.840
gathers that the Predators may be attempting to&nbsp;
improve themselves through genetic engineering.

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:22.160
"If I'm right, it means they're&nbsp;
trying to upgrade themselves."

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:26.480
The group is discovered and interrogated by&nbsp;
government agent Will Traeger and his agents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:30.960
who have taken Rory upon discovering that he&nbsp;
gained knowledge of the aliens. McKenna and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:36.080
the escaped soldiers ambush Traeger's agents&nbsp;
before being ambushed by the larger Predator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:41.200
in turn. The larger Predator takes Rory to his&nbsp;
ship, seeing his unique mind as an adaptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:46.240
worthy of genetic incorporation and attempts to&nbsp;
take off before McKenna's allies ground the ship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:51.280
and the Yautja is killed with its own weapons.&nbsp;
The film ends with the discovery that the first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:56.400
smaller Predator was transporting a human-sized&nbsp;
piece of advanced 'Predator' armor to Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:58.080
labeled a 'Predator killer' suit.

00:16:59.585 --> 00:17:02.480
"The Predator" reveals that at&nbsp;
some point the Yautja species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:07.440
began genetic engineering to incorporate the&nbsp;
strengths of other species into their DNA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:11.600
The two Predators we encounter are both&nbsp;
genetically engineered but have wildly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:16.560
different attributes. Additionally, the&nbsp;
film implicitly folds "Alien vs. Predator"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:21.040
into the "Predator" franchise through its&nbsp;
highlighting a weapon exclusive to that film.

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:26.080
We also discover that the human/Yautja hybrid&nbsp;
was on a mission to deliver the advanced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:31.520
human-sized 'Predator Killer' armor, presumably&nbsp;
to give humanity an advantage against a possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.520 --> 00:17:37.040
future invasion. The reason for this gift may be&nbsp;
due to sympathies resulting from that specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:42.000
Predator being partially human, but we at least&nbsp;
know that the intentional hybridization of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:46.160
Yautja species is yet another major&nbsp;
development in the Predator mythos.

00:17:47.266 --> 00:17:52.000
"The Predator" is notable for its intriguing&nbsp;
suggestion that the Yautja may be interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:55.280
in the future of humanity on a&nbsp;
larger scale beyond the hunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:57.920
including potential genetic incorporation.

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:03.200
The film was a relatively modest success&nbsp;
by Hollywood standards, delivering a $159&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:09.520
million global box office on an $88 million&nbsp;
budget. Critically, it wasn't as successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:15.440
with a rating of 33% on Rotten Tomatoes. Believe&nbsp;
it or not, this still makes it the third highest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.440 --> 00:18:21.280
rated film in the entire series. Overall, we have&nbsp;
yet to see the full impact of the film's legacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.280 --> 00:18:25.600
including the genetic engineering and&nbsp;
the potential for a larger Yautja threat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:30.480
but its contribution to their cinematic&nbsp;
mythos is definitely worth thinking about.

